                    <!-- Tab 2: How Tests are Generated -->
                    <div class="tab-pane fade" id="guide-howgen" role="tabpanel">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2">Under the Hood: Microsoft Roslyn AST Code Analysis</h6>
                        <p class="text-muted small">
                            Unlike basic regex-based tools, this platform uses the official <strong>Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp (Roslyn)</strong> engine to understand your C# syntax and semantic structure deeply:
                        </p>

                        <div class="row g-3 mb-3">
                            <div class="col-md-6">
                                <div class="p-3 border rounded-3 bg-white">
                                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-primary small mb-1"><i class="ri-node-tree me-1"></i>1. Dependency Injection Extraction</h6>
                                    <p class="text-muted small mb-0">
                                        Roslyn scans constructors for injected interfaces (e.g., <code>IReturnInvoiceService</code>, <code>IWorkflow</code>, <code>IConfiguration</code>).
                                        It generates <code>Mock&lt;T&gt;</code> fields for each dependency and instantiates the System Under Test (SUT) with <code>_mockDep.Object</code>.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="col-md-6">
                                <div class="p-3 border rounded-3 bg-white">
                                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-info small mb-1"><i class="ri-git-branch-line me-1"></i>2. Method & Branch Exploration</h6>
                                    <p class="text-muted small mb-0">
                                        For every public method, the engine inspects parameters, return types (synchronous and <code>async Task&lt;T&gt;</code>), and conditional statements (<code>if</code>, <code>switch</code>, null-coalescing <code>??</code>).
                                        It generates test cases targeting both happy paths and branch conditions.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="bg-dark text-white p-3 rounded-3 font-monospace small">
                            <span class="text-secondary">// Example Generated Architecture</span><br/>
                            <span class="text-info">public class</span> <span class="text-warning">WorkEvidenceServiceTests</span><br/>
                            {<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-secondary">// 1. Mocks automatically declared for every injected interface</span><br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-info">private readonly</span> Mock&lt;IWorkEvidenceRepository&gt; _mockWorkEvidenceRepository;<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-info">private readonly</span> WorkEvidenceService _service;<br/><br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-secondary">// 2. Constructor sets up mocks and injects into SUT</span><br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-info">public</span> <span class="text-warning">WorkEvidenceServiceTests</span>()<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;{<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;_mockWorkEvidenceRepository = <span class="text-info">new</span> Mock&lt;IWorkEvidenceRepository&gt;();<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;_service = <span class="text-info">new</span> WorkEvidenceService(_mockWorkEvidenceRepository.Object);<br/>
                            &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}<br/>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Tab 3: How Tests Execute -->
                    <div class="tab-pane fade" id="guide-howtest" role="tabpanel">
                        <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark mb-2">How the Generated Tests Actually Run & Validate Code</h6>
                        <p class="text-muted small">Every generated test follows the industry-standard <strong>AAA (Arrange-Act-Assert)</strong> pattern to guarantee deterministic, isolated unit testing:</p>

                        <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                            <table class="table table-bordered table-sm small">
                                <thead class="table-light">
                                    <tr>
                                        <th style="width: 120px;">Phase</th>
                                        <th style="width: 200px;">Mechanism</th>
                                        <th>What It Does</th>
                                    </tr>
                                </thead>
                                <tbody>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="badge bg-primary">Arrange</span></td>
                                        <td><strong>Moq Setups & Test Fixtures</strong></td>
                                        <td>Prepares input variables and programs mock behavior (e.g. <code>_mockRepo.Setup(x => x.GetAsync(It.IsAny&lt;long&gt;())).ReturnsAsync(mockEntity)</code>). The test does NOT require a real database or network connection!</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Act</span></td>
                                        <td><strong>Method Execution</strong></td>
                                        <td>Invokes the actual target method on the service: <code>var result = await _service.AddWorkEvidenceDetails(payload);</code>.</td>
                                    </tr>
                                    <tr>
                                        <td><span class="badge bg-success">Assert</span></td>
                                        <td><strong>FluentAssertions</strong></td>
                                        <td>Verifies expected return values, state changes, or exceptions thrown (e.g. <code>result.Should().BeTrue();</code> or <code>_mockRepo.Verify(x => x.SaveAsync(...), Times.Once);</code>).</td>
                                    </tr>
                                </tbody>
                            </table>
                        </div>

                        <div class="p-3 border rounded-3 bg-white">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold text-dark small mb-1"><i class="ri-play-circle-line text-warning me-1"></i>Running via 'Execute dotnet test' or Visual Studio Test Explorer</h6>
                            <p class="text-muted small mb-0">
                                Once saved or downloaded, tests can be run directly inside Visual Studio Test Explorer (Ctrl+R, A), via the built-in <strong>Test Runner & Coverage</strong> tab on this page, or inside automated CI/CD pipelines (e.g. Azure Pipelines: <code>dotnet test --collect:"XPlat Code Coverage"</code>).
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

